king almost all my chips (or all of them) on a hand this weak is incredibly foolish. It\'s mathematically the worst decision. With only 12 hands left, I need to preserve my chips to play a more favorable hand.\n\n**Therefore, I am Folding.** The prompt asks for `Evaluation`, `Reason`, and `Answer`.\n\nEvaluation: My hand is very weak, and the opponent\'s all-in bet strongly suggests a strong hand. The expected value of calling is negative, making folding the correct play.\n\nReason: To preserve my stack and maximize my chances of winning in later hands, folding is the mathematically superior decision.\n\nAnswer: -1\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: Your hand (3 of Hearts, 8 of Clubs) is an unsuited 8-high, which is statistically one of the weakest starting hands in Heads-Up Texas Hold\'em (often referred to as "trash"). Against a random hand, you have approximately 32% equity, meaning you will lose roughly two-thirds of the time at showdown. The opponent has made a massive raise (effectively an All-In), requiring you to risk your entire stack to see the community cards.\n\nReason: To justify calling an All-In, you generally need a hand with strong equity (like a pair, high cards, or suited connectors) or pot odds that favor a call. With 8-3 offsuit, the Expected Value (EV) of calling is highly negative. You are extremely likely to lose this hand. Since chips reset every hand or the goal is to maximize cumulative score, taking a high-risk, low-probability gamble here is mathematically unsound. Folding minimizes your loss to just the blinds (if posted) rather than risking 19,900 chips on a bad hand.\n\nAnswer: -1'}
